DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggeettppiidd() returns the process ID (PID) of the calling process.  (This is
       often used by routines that generate unique temporary filenames.)

       ggeettppppiidd()  returns the process ID of the parent of the calling process.
       This will be either the ID of the process that created this process us‐
       ing ffoorrkk(), or, if that process has already terminated, the ID  of  the
       process  to which this process has been reparented (either iinniitt(1) or a
       "subreaper" process defined via the pprrccttll(2) PPRR__SSEETT__CCHHIILLDD__SSUUBBRREEAAPPEERR op‐
       eration).

EERRRROORRSS
       These functions are always successful.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       On Alpha, instead of a pair of ggeettppiidd() and ggeettppppiidd() system  calls,  a
       single  ggeettxxppiidd()  system call is provided, which returns a pair of PID
       and parent PID.  The glibc ggeettppiidd()  and  ggeettppppiidd()  wrapper  functions
       transparently  deal  with  this.   See ssyyssccaallll(2) for details regarding
       register mapping.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       POSIX.1‐2024.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
