   CC lliibbrraarryy//kkeerrnneell ddiiffffeerreenncceess
       From glibc 2.3.4 up to and including  glibc  2.24,  the  glibc  wrapper
       function for ggeettppiidd() cached PIDs, with the goal of avoiding additional
       system  calls  when a process calls ggeettppiidd() repeatedly.  Normally this
       caching was invisible, but its correct operation relied on  support  in
       the wrapper functions for ffoorrkk(2), vvffoorrkk(2), and cclloonnee(2): if an appli‐
       cation  bypassed  the  glibc  wrappers  for these system calls by using
       ssyyssccaallll(2), then a call to ggeettppiidd() in the child would return the wrong
       value (to be precise: it would return the PID of the  parent  process).
       In  addition,  there  were  cases where ggeettppiidd() could return the wrong
       value even when invoking cclloonnee(2) via the glibc wrapper function.  (For
       a discussion of one such case, see BUGS in cclloonnee(2).)  Furthermore, the
       complexity of the caching code had been the source of a few bugs within
       glibc over the years.

       Because of the aforementioned problems, since glibc 2.25, the PID cache
       is removed: calls to ggeettppiidd() always invoke  the  actual  system  call,
       rather than returning a cached value.

NNOOTTEESS
       If  the  caller’s parent is in a different PID namespace (see ppiidd__nnaammee‐‐
       ssppaacceess(7)), ggeettppppiidd() returns 0.

       From a kernel perspective, the PID (which  is  shared  by  all  of  the
       threads  in  a  multithreaded  process)  is sometimes also known as the
       thread group ID (TGID).  This  contrasts  with  the  kernel  thread  ID
       (TID),  which is unique for each thread.  For further details, see ggeett‐‐
       ttiidd(2) and the discussion of the CCLLOONNEE__TTHHRREEAADD flag in cclloonnee(2).

